Process Management Strategy – Linux

A faulty process consuming 90% of CPU resources can slow down the entire Linux system. Since restarting the system is not allowed, the administrator
can identify, monitor, and control the process using Linux process-management commands.
CODE:
#!/bin/bash
echo "======================================"
echo "     LINUX PROCESS MANAGEMENT"
echo "======================================"
echo ""
echo "1. Checking CPU-intensive processes..."
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -6

echo ""
read -p "Enter the PID of the faulty process: " PID

# Check whether the process exists
if ps -p "$PID" > /dev/null
then
    echo ""
    echo "2. Process identified successfully."

    echo "Process Details:"
    ps -p "$PID" -o PID,USER,COMM,%CPU,%MEM,STAT

    echo ""
    echo "3. Monitoring CPU usage..."
    sleep 2
    ps -p "$PID" -o PID,COMM,%CPU,%MEM

    echo ""
    echo "4. Reducing CPU priority..."

    sudo renice +10 -p "$PID"

    echo ""
    echo "5. Updated process information:"
    ps -p "$PID" -o PID,COMM,%CPU,%MEM,NI,STAT

    echo ""
    echo "The faulty process has been given lower CPU priority."
    echo "Critical services can now receive more CPU resources."

    echo ""
    read -p "Do you want to terminate the process? (y/n): " CHOICE

    if [ "$CHOICE" = "y" ]
    then
        sudo kill "$PID"
        echo "Process $PID terminated safely."
    else
        echo "Process continues with reduced priority."
    fi

else
    echo ""
    echo "Process with PID $PID was not found."
fi

echo ""
echo "======================================"
echo "Process management completed."
echo "======================================"

ps aux --sort=-%cpu     – Finds processes using the highest CPU.
ps -p PID               – Checks whether the selected process is running.
sleep                   – Provides a short interval for monitoring.
renice +10              – Reduces the CPU priority of the faulty process.
kill PID                – Terminates the process without restarting the system.
if condition            – Prevents commands from being executed if the process does not exist.

Conclusion:
This strategy safely manages a faulty high-CPU process without rebooting the Linux system. ps identifies the process, renice reduces its CPU priority,
and kill provides a final option if the process continues causing problems. This helps maintain the performance of critical services.

